# The posture checks known to fail on each form (make check; test/boot):
#
#   FORM ID...
#   ARCH ID...
#
# A form takes the * line, or, built with DEV=1 as make check builds every
# form, the dev line; a FORM line, and an ARCH line (x86_64, aarch64), add
# their ids to it, for that form and on that architecture. Each is a weakness still
# open (docs/security.md, "Not yet"): test/boot fails a machine on any
# other failure, and on any of these that starts passing, so this list
# cannot claim less, or more, than the machines hold.
#
# No form carries a shell but those that log people in (sshd, lima,
# prod-ssh, none of which make check boots as it ships) and DEV=1 builds,
# which add busybox-full: its shell, awk, wget, nc and telnetd.
#
# On x86_64, modify_ldt(2) answers until the seal's seccomp filter denies it
# (design/lockdown.md); ia32_emulation=0 has closed int 0x80.
#
# Every form, by choice (docs/security.md, "Not done, by choice"): nothing
# here may slow what the machines run, a database or ../scan.
#   kernel-memory-hardening   init_on_alloc and init_on_free zero every
#                             page and object, which costs allocation-heavy
#                             work; the rest come with them or not at all
#   kernel-cpu-mitigations    forcing the store-bypass mitigation slows
#                             every process; ? because it is the host's CPU
#                             that has the flaw or not
#   network-rp-filter         strict mode must first be shown to work with
#                             fence's policy routing, whose rules make the
#                             reverse lookup look locally sent
#   network-ipv6-ra           IPv6 is on, and router advertisements are how
#                             most networks give it a route
#
# ?ID: may fail or not, as the host decides.

dev network-no-login programs-no-downloaders programs-no-interpreters programs-no-shell kernel-memory-hardening ?kernel-cpu-mitigations network-rp-filter network-ipv6-ra
* kernel-memory-hardening ?kernel-cpu-mitigations network-rp-filter network-ipv6-ra
x86_64 kernel-legacy
# qemu-host has KVM, as its form allows, wherever its host lends it the
# hardware: EL2 on aarch64 (Apple M3 and later, QEMU 11), VMX or SVM on
# x86_64.
qemu-host ?kernel-no-hypervisor
